CORE
main.cpp
--cpp20 --unwind 6 --no-unwinding-assertions
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ size is 2 after erase_if removes one element: SUCCESS$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^.*invariant violation.*$
^.*found no match for symbol.*$
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
End-to-end: std::erase_if over a populated std::vector (real libstdc++) removes
the single element equal to 2 from {1,2,3}, leaving size() == 2.

This is the culmination of the unified variadic pack-expansion rework
(doc/architectural/cpp-variadic-pack-expansion-rework.md).  std::erase_if passes
its predicate as std::ref(__pred) to std::__remove_if, so the predicate is
invoked through reference_wrapper::operator() -- a variadic *member* template of
a class template whose dependent return type is invoke_result_t<T&, _Args...>.

Two cooperating fixes made this work (both also covered by header-free tests):
  * [temp.deduct.call]/1 + [temp.variadic]/5: a deduced function parameter pack
    is recorded as a pack (pack_args_map / pack_size_map), not only as a scalar
    type_map binding, so the dependent return type expands the pack and drops
    the `...` marker (cpp11_decltype_pack_in_class_template).
  * a non-constructor member template of a class template now binds its
    enclosing class's template arguments -- both during deduction and, via
    ID_C_class on the deduced function type, during instantiation -- so a
    member whose signature depends on the class parameters (here `T` in
    invoke_result<T&, _Args...>) resolves and instantiates.

Bounded with --unwind to keep the test fast.
